{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgqkfqB2C791NUUhPzR18","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I couldn't open the source, so this diagnosis is inferred from the retrieved call graph and the symbol line ranges.\n\n`resetPassword` decodes the hash, loads the user with `findById`, and then calls `verifyAsync`. It passes `getForgotSecret` as the signing secret. After that it deletes the user's sessions and calls `UsersService.update`. Nothing in that flow marks the token as used.\n\nSingle use normally comes from tying the token's signing secret to state that changes after a reset, usually the user's current password hash. If `getForgotSecret` returns a secret that doesn't change when the password changes, the same JWT stays valid until it expires. That matches what you saw. Any other one-time mechanism, such as storing and deleting the token, would have to live in `resetPassword`.\n\n<br>"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16028,"cache_read_input_tokens":791,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16028},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"587fc5b6-f5cc-42fb-b963-db843edeb121","uuid":"8530ce7f-069d-432b-accb-4ede68ced246","timestamp":"2026-11-08T16:11:12.599Z","request_id":"req_011CgqkfpmDGgP9xAPf5LsMc"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgqkfqB2C791NUUhPzR18","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016h6tuZZp7bWLiBo8EpkDmf","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"candidates":[{"file":"app/src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.getForgotSecret","start_line":588,"end_line":597,"explanation":"The secret used to verify the forgot-password JWT most likely doesn't include per-user state that changes after a reset, such as the current password hash. The token therefore stays valid after the password is changed and can be reused until it expires. I couldn't read this code, so this is inferred from the call graph."},{"file":"app/src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.resetPassword","start_line":367,"end_line":420,"explanation":"The method verifies the hash and updates the password and sessions. It never invalidates the token afterwards, and it doesn't check that the token was issued against the user's current password state. A replayed hash therefore passes verification again."}],"fix":{"file":"app/src/auth/auth.service.ts","description":"Make the reset token single-use. Derive the verification secret from the user's current password hash, for example `forgotSecret + user.password`, in `getForgotSecret` and in the signing step in `forgotPassword`. Once the password changes, the old token then fails verification. Alternatively, store the reset token or a nonce and delete it inside `resetPassword` after a successful update."}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16028,"cache_read_input_tokens":791,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16028},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,